\newlabel{fig-shapiro-trap}{{2.11}{76}{The Shapiro-Wilk trap illustrated. Left: with small samples (n = 5 per group), the test fails to detect clear non-normality, returning p \textgreater {} 0.05 in the majority of cases. Right: with large samples (n = 200 per group), the test rejects normality almost universally, even for data generated from an exactly normal distribution with only a trivial added skew}{figure.caption.16}{}}
